TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E is presented as a likely 2025 launch vehicle, but Vega E was not an operational launcher and did not make its first launch in 2024; this undermines the proposed launch plan. The stated future launch schedule and rideshare availability are not substantiated."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The claim that two launch windows per day follow because the launch-site latitude is lower than the target inclination is oversimplified. Actual launch azimuth, range-safety constraints, target RAAN and rideshare orbit determine feasible windows."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"At 600 km altitude, a near-circular orbit has a period of about 97 minutes, but the report does not give a period or demonstrate that the selected RAAN and launch date can deliver the stated elements. RAAN is not determined simply by the launch facility location."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says the orbit has a constant sun angle and provides uniform illumination, but does not specify local solar time of the ascending/descending node. SSO fixes local solar time at a given latitude; it does not guarantee constant illumination conditions for every imaging pass."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A single 600 km spacecraft cannot image the entire 250 km-wide reef at adequate resolution merely by selecting this altitude. Sensor swath, optical resolution, pointing, cloud cover and revisit/coverage requirements are not specified or analysed."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory is identified as a radio astronomy facility, but the report does not establish that it has suitable spacecraft TT&C antennas, frequency authorizations, or operational control capability. Pass access alone does not demonstrate a usable communications link."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table includes a duplicated access entry and a first listed interval with no access number; the claim of frequent contact is not reconciled with the reported gaps between passes or the time window represented."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The report first says an EOL orbital manoeuvre occurs, then states the spacecraft has no thrusters and relies on a passive drag sail. A deployable drag sail is a plausible concept but requires design, reliability and lifetime/deorbit analysis; none is provided."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report describes a circular orbit as having undefined argument of perigee and true anomaly, but does not give a fully specified nonsingular alternative set including a reference epoch and phase; the stated argument of latitude alone is insufficient for reproducibility."}],"overall":"The submission presents a coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept with substantial mission-section coverage and STK outputs. Its strongest limitations are an infeasible launch assumption, insufficiently substantiated orbit and communications design, and an incomplete EOL plan. The report’s figures and referencing also need more professional treatment.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report explains the reef-monitoring mission and its Australian environmental rationale, justifies a single spacecraft, and identifies likely government/science users. Operator and customers are not clearly distinguished, and the rationale and user discussion have limited supporting references, so this is at the lower end of Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The ZYZZsat however, does not require such constant coverage as the reef bleaching events occur over days and weeks. Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report addresses launch, orbit and elements, manoeuvres, ground access, duration and EOL, and includes STK-derived material. However, the proposed Vega E is not a credible 2025 launch choice; launch compatibility and selection are not demonstrated. Orbit choices and access results have limited validation, ground-station suitability is not established, and the EOL plan is incomplete and internally inconsistent. No software files are evident.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the principal design choices and notes launch uncertainty, but mostly restates the proposal. It does not draw detailed conclusions from quantified performance evidence or resolve the significant feasibility and EOL uncertainties.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable engineering structure, executive summary, bibliography, and useful plots and data. Presentation is weakened by inconsistent figure labelling/captions, malformed or incomplete references, use of Wikipedia as a source, and grammar/wording errors. Figures are not consistently identified by number and title.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The following ground track details both ascending and descending orbital paths, illustrating the abundance of ground station contact time"}]}]}
